Hi Brian,

I spent the evening yesterday trying to compile Kraken under Windows and finally
succeeded with your help and help from internet. Thanks for the great code and
nicely written mex wrapper.

Attached is the mexw64 file. Please add it to the library if you find it
suitable. I haven't done extensive testings on it, but 3 test examples are
plotting something very reasonable.

Here is the step-by step guide how to create this file:

1) Set up. Windows 7 64 bit, Intel XEON X5482 CPU, Microsoft Visual Studio 2010
and Intel Fortran Compiler XE 12.0 integrated into Visual Studio(I also
succeeded with Microsoft Visual Studio 2005 and Intel Visual Fortran 10.1; but
you need different libraries; see below). Note that Matlab R2010 (as of
02/04/2011) does not support Intel Fortran Compiler XE 12.0

2) Compile all fortran routines from Visual Studio environment with Fortran
compiler. Compile modules first (krakmod and sdrdrmod). The trick is to set
Fortran Libraries compiler option to "Multithread DLL (/libs:dll /threads)".
Otherwise linker will complain about missing main program. Make sure that you
have chosen x64 platform. I also have chosen "Release" configuration, set
Optimization to "Maximize Speed plus Higher Level Optimizations (/O3), set
Parallelization to "Yes (/Qparallel)" and set Default Real KIND to 8 (in
Fortran Data options; I am not sure whether this matters or not, seems to work
either way). After this step is complete you will end up with 16 .obj files.

3) In mkrak.c replace kraken_ with KRAKEN (remove underscore, case sensitive!).
Alternatively there is an option to compile fortran codes with lower-case
names, but I have not tried it. Otherwise C++ linker will not be able to find
objects created by Fortran Compiler

4) Make sure that MEX compiler is set to Microsoft Visual C++ 2010
(run mex -setup).  From Matlab run the following command:
mex -L"C:\Program Files (x86)\Intel\oneAPI\compiler\2025.0\lib"
mkrak_jb.c...
     bcimp.o kraken.o kuping.o readin.o sinvitd.o splinec.o
twersk.o zbrentx.o...
     errout.o krakmod.o mergev.o sdrdrmod.o sorti.o subtab.o
weight.o zsecx.o...
     -l"ifconsol" -l"libifcoremd" -l"libifportmd" -l"libiomp5md" -l"libmmd"
-l"libirc"  -l"svml_disp"...
     -l"libmatmul" -l"libmmt" -l"libdecimal"
Here -L"C:...." is the path to Fortran libraries. I could not find a way how to
add this path to mex linking options, so I manually pointed mex to each library.
With Visual Studio 2005 and Intel Visual Fortran 10.1 you need slightly
different set of libraries. An easy way to figure out which ones it to link
without libraries and add them one by one from error messages (you only need
about 10). Alternatively, it should be possible to set mex to look for these
libraries automatically.

Done.

Ilya.

